=========================================
C++11 TYPE TRAITS
INTERVIEW NOTES
=========================================


1. What are Type Traits?

Type traits are C++11 utilities provided by the <type_traits> header.

They allow us to inspect properties of types at compile time.

Examples:

- Is this type an integer?
- Is this type a pointer?
- Are two types the same?
- Is this type a class?


Header:

#include <type_traits>


=========================================
1. std::is_same
=========================================

Purpose:

Checks whether two types are the same.


Example:

#include<iostream>
#include<type_traits>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    cout << is_same<int,int>::value << endl;

    cout << is_same<int,double>::value << endl;
}


Output:

1
0


C++17:

is_same_v<int,int>


-----------------------------------------


2. std::is_pointer
=========================================

Purpose:

Checks whether a type is a pointer.


Example:

#include<iostream>
#include<type_traits>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int x = 10;

    cout << is_pointer<int*>::value << endl;

    cout << is_pointer<int>::value << endl;
}


Output:

1
0


Real use with template:

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    if constexpr(is_pointer<T>::value)
    {
        cout << "Pointer value: " << *value;
    }
    else
    {
        cout << "Normal value: " << value;
    }
}


-----------------------------------------


3. std::is_integral
=========================================

Purpose:

Checks whether a type is an integer type.


Integral types:

int
char
bool
short
long


Example:

#include<iostream>
#include<type_traits>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    cout << is_integral<int>::value << endl;

    cout << is_integral<double>::value << endl;
}


Output:

1
0


Template usage:

template<typename T>
void check(T value)
{
    if constexpr(is_integral<T>::value)
    {
        cout << "Integer type";
    }
    else
    {
        cout << "Not integer";
    }
}


