=========================================
Type Traits With Templates
=========================================


Example:

#include<iostream>
#include<type_traits>

using namespace std;


template<typename T>
void display(T value)
{
    if constexpr(is_pointer<T>::value)
    {
        cout << "Pointer: " << *value << endl;
    }
    else
    {
        cout << "Value: " << value << endl;
    }
}


int main()
{
    int x = 100;

    display(x);

    display(&x);
}


Output:

Value: 100
Pointer: 100


=========================================
C++11 vs C++17 Syntax
=========================================


C++11:

is_pointer<T>::value

is_integral<T>::value

is_same<T,U>::value


C++17:

is_pointer_v<T>

is_integral_v<T>

is_same_v<T,U>


Example:


C++11:

if constexpr(is_pointer<T>::value)


C++17:

if constexpr(is_pointer_v<T>)


=========================================
Interview Important Points
=========================================


Q. What are type traits?

Answer:

"Type traits are C++11 utilities from <type_traits> that provide
compile-time information about types. They are commonly used in
template programming."


-----------------------------------------


Q. Are type traits C++17 features?

Answer:

No.

Type traits were introduced in C++11.

C++17 added cleaner _v variable template shortcuts.


-----------------------------------------


Q. Why are type traits useful?

Answer:

- Compile-time type checking
- Generic programming
- Template specialization
- if constexpr decisions
- STL implementation


